Id: fsys002w
Severity: WARN
Category: filesystem
Check: check_suid
Fix: Rebuild the program with absolute paths, or take the setuid bit off.
References: strings(1); system(3)
Controls: CIS 4.1; NIST AC-6, CM-7; ISO 8.2, 8.18; CE Secure configuration

The program is a setuid executable that no installed package makes
setuid (fsys004a), and it appears to contain relative pathnames (do
not start with a '/').  This often represents a security hole in the
program.  These relative pathnames can be caused by system()* or
popen()* calls which do not use full pathnames to the executable, or,
on systems which support dynamic linking, relative pathnames
indicating the directories containing the libraries.  In any case,
these need to be checked.  The programs packages ship setuid are not
looked at: they all contain "../" (sudo, pkexec and ssh-keysign name
it to refuse it), so the test said nothing there.

*Note:  system() and popen() should *never* be used from a program
        which is executing with privileges.
